Final score = 0.5 × rank(score 1) + 0.5 × rank(score 2)



Emphasize that rank-combining avoids giving more weight to the score with larger raw units. It also asks both methods to vote on which properties are suspicious.

The Central Limit Theorem
The central limit theorem helps explain why many estimators have approximately normal sampling distributions when the sample size is large. To study this idea, I repeatedly simulate the estimator theta_hat under different sample sizes.

def simulate_theta_hat(n, pi_A=0.22, pi_B=0.18, reps=1000):
    vals = []
    for _ in range(reps):
        A_sim = np.random.binomial(1, pi_A, n)
        B_sim = np.random.binomial(1, pi_B, n)
        vals.append(A_sim.mean() - B_sim.mean())
    return np.array(vals)

sample_sizes = [25, 50, 100, 500]
simulated_results = {n_i: simulate_theta_hat(n_i) for n_i in sample_sizes}










With small sample sizes, the distribution of the estimator is rougher and more dispersed. As the sample size increases, the distribution becomes more concentrated and more bell-shaped. This is the central limit theorem at work.
